  "query_preview": "..Tranquil weather conditions are taking place across\nnorthern Arizona early this morning as mostly zonal flow remains\noverhead. Temperatures this afternoon are forecast to be on the\nwarm side for this time of year, 5-10 degrees above average, as\nhumidity continues to be below 15% across the region. Expect\ntypical west-southwest breezes, 10-20 mph, with clear skies.\n\nAs we head into the weekend, weather conditions worsen thanks to a\nlow pressure trough dropping southward from the Intermountain\nWest. Temperatures will begin a cooling trend on Saturday and will\ncontinue through Monday. Afternoon humidity looks to increase\nslightly each day with improved recoveries each night, however,\nconditions still look to remain dry as forecast minimum humidities\nare in the 15-20% range for Sunday...just at or slightly above \ncritical levels. Near critical fire weather conditions are likely\non Saturday with 10-15% afternoon humidity and southwest winds of\n20-35 mph. Confidence continues to increase in more widespread \ncritical fire weather conditions on Sunday, with peak wind gusts \nof 30-50 mph. Even with the slight increase in humidity and \nchances for rain/snow showers to develop along the AZ/UT border \nSunday afternoon through early Monday morning, much of northern \nArizona should remain dry. \n\nThe coolest temperatures of the new week will take place on Monday\nas the cold front, accompanying the trough, heads eastward\nresulting in 5-10 degree below average temperatures and breezy\nwesterly winds, 15-25 mph. The trough begins to lift towards the\neast on Tuesday as heights increase west to east across northern\nArizona. Mild temperatures return for much of next week along with\ndry conditions and afternoon humidity below 15%. Plan on southwest\nbreezes each afternoon, 10-20 mph.",
